[h1]Harmony[/h1]
[b]Europa Universalis IV, made better from within.[/b] Chinese, Japanese and Korean everywhere, no restarts, a faster game, fewer bugs, and mods that can do far more, with the game's rules left as they are.

[b]Note: subscribing alone does not activate Harmony[/b] — after subscribing, copy one file ([b]version.dll[/b]) into the game's folder once (updates after that are automatic).

[img]https://images.steamusercontent.com/ugc/17691956205021936994/F80E950590FA30D9515A932BDF5523EFB9A353C0/[/img]

Your translation mod is still what translates the game; translation mods made for the old double-byte patch, and the saves played with them, work as they are.

[h2]Installing[/h2]
Harmony is a program that runs inside the game, so one file has to go into the game's folder by hand, once.

[olist]
[*]Subscribe to Harmony on this page.
[*]In Steam, right-click Europa Universalis IV and choose [b]Manage → Browse local files[/b]. This opens the game's folder (keep it open).
[*]Open the Paradox launcher, go to [b]Mod library[/b], find [b]Harmony[/b], and click [b]… → Show in folder[/b]. This opens Harmony's folder. (If Harmony does not appear there yet, restart Steam; you can also open [b]steamapps\workshop\content\236850\3837775887[/b] in the same Steam library as the game.)
[*]Copy [b]version.dll[/b] from Harmony's folder into the game's folder, next to [b]eu4.exe[/b]. (Windows may hide file extensions by default, showing them as [b]version[/b] and [b]eu4[/b]. If the game's folder already has a [b]version.dll[/b] — for example from an older CJK patch — choose [b]Replace[/b]; if you want to keep the old file to switch back later, rename or move it aside first.)
[/olist]

Start the game from the Paradox launcher as usual. Harmony needs no place in your playset; mods built on it are enabled there as any mod. The main menu now has two buttons in its corner, [b]Harmony settings[/b] and [b]Harmony mods[/b].

Steam keeps Harmony up to date. When an update includes a new version.dll, Harmony updates the copy in the game's folder on startup and restarts the game automatically.

[h2]Uninstalling[/h2]
Unsubscribe from Harmony, and the game runs as it always did. To leave nothing behind, delete version.dll from the game's folder too, and put back any backup of an earlier version.dll you kept.

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*]Europa Universalis IV 1.37.5, on Windows.
[*]Single player only.
[/list]

[h2]About your saves[/h2]
[list]
[*]Names you type in a game played with Harmony are saved as UTF-8, and show as garbage without it.
[*]A mod that keeps data of its own writes it into the save; such a save is not promised to load without Harmony.
[*]With Harmony, the game's volume sliders go as the square of their position, so each step sounds about as large. Volumes saved below 100 sound quieter than before; set them once again.
[/list]

[h2]When something goes wrong[/h2]
In the main menu, open Harmony's settings, go to [b]About[/b] and press [b]Save troubleshooting files to the desktop[/b]: it packs Harmony's logs, the game's logs, its last crash folders and your settings and playset (no saves) into one zip file on your desktop. Report a problem on [url=https://github.com/KisinTheFlame/eu4-harmony/issues]GitHub Issues[/url], with that file attached.

[h2]For mod authors[/h2]
A mod built on Harmony is an ordinary Paradox mod with Lua scripts that read the game and act in it as the player does, add buttons and windows in the game's look, and run in a sandbox that reaches beyond the game only as far as the mod declares. How to write one is in the [url=https://github.com/KisinTheFlame/eu4-harmony]README on GitHub[/url].
